---
import BaseHead from "../components/BaseHead.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, LOCK_BASE, SERVICES, SITE_TITLE, X_HANDLE, X_URL } from "../consts";

const relay = SERVICES.find((s) => s.name === "NanoRelay");
const relayIsLive = relay?.status === "live";

const setup = `export KEY=np_live_...              # the key you were sent
export PULSE=https://pulse.nano-api.com
export RELAY=https://relay.nano-api.com
export LOCK=https://lock.nano-api.com
auth=(-H "Authorization: Bearer $KEY" -H 'content-type: application/json')`;

const firstPing = `curl -X POST "$PULSE/v1/ping/nightly-backup?interval=86400&grace=3600" "\${auth[@]}"`;

const crontab = `0 3 * * * /usr/local/bin/backup.sh && curl -fsS -X POST \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $KEY" \\
  https://pulse.nano-api.com/v1/ping/nightly-backup`;

const pingPayload = `curl -X POST "$PULSE/v1/ping/nightly-backup" "\${auth[@]}" \\
  -d '{"rows_exported": 12043, "duration_s": 94}'`;

const alerts = `curl -X PATCH "$PULSE/v1/monitors/nightly-backup" "\${auth[@]}" \\
  -d '{"alert_webhook_url": "https://hooks.slack.com/services/..."}'`;

const proveIt = `# 1. a monitor with the shortest allowed window
curl -X POST "$PULSE/v1/monitors" "\${auth[@]}" \\
  -d '{"slug":"alert-test","expected_interval_seconds":30,"grace_period_seconds":0,
       "alert_webhook_url":"https://hooks.slack.com/services/..."}'

# 2. one ping, so it has something to miss
curl -X POST "$PULSE/v1/ping/alert-test" "\${auth[@]}"

# 3. let the window pass, then run the sweep yourself
sleep 35
curl -X POST "$PULSE/v1/checks/run" "\${auth[@]}"      # -> 🔴 in Slack

# 4. ping again to recover, then clean up
curl -X POST "$PULSE/v1/ping/alert-test" "\${auth[@]}"  # -> 🟢 in Slack
curl -X DELETE "$PULSE/v1/monitors/alert-test" "\${auth[@]}"`;

const inspect = `curl -s "$PULSE/v1/monitors/nightly-backup" "\${auth[@]}" | jq`;

const reportFailure = `curl -X POST "$PULSE/v1/ping/nightly-backup?status=fail" "\${auth[@]}"`;

const pauseMonitor = `curl -X PATCH "$PULSE/v1/monitors/nightly-backup" "\${auth[@]}" -d '{"paused": true}'`;

const createSchedule = `curl -X POST "$RELAY/v1/schedules" "\${auth[@]}" -d '{
  "slug": "nightly-report",
  "cron": "0 3 * * *",
  "timezone": "Europe/Oslo",
  "url": "https://api.example.com/jobs/nightly-report",
  "body": {"source": "relay"},
  "max_attempts": 3,
  "timeout_seconds": 30,
  "alert_webhook_url": "https://hooks.slack.com/services/..."
}'`;

const runNow = `curl -X POST "$RELAY/v1/schedules/nightly-report/run" "\${auth[@]}" | jq .run`;

const runNowOutput = `{
  "outcome": "ok",
  "status_code": 200,
  "duration_ms": 412,
  "response_excerpt": "{\\"ok\\":true}",
  "error": null,
  "attempts": 1,
  "run_id": "752cb057-e1f9-4512-812d-8100ea1cee8d"
}`;

const scheduleHistory = `curl -s "$RELAY/v1/schedules/nightly-report" "\${auth[@]}" | jq .recent_runs`;

const pauseSchedule = `curl -X PATCH "$RELAY/v1/schedules/nightly-report" "\${auth[@]}" -d '{"paused": true}'
curl -X PATCH "$RELAY/v1/schedules/nightly-report" "\${auth[@]}" -d '{"cron": "*/15 * * * *"}'
curl -X DELETE "$RELAY/v1/schedules/nightly-report" "\${auth[@]}"`;

const both = `# 1. Relay calls your endpoint every hour.
curl -X POST "$RELAY/v1/schedules" "\${auth[@]}" -d '{
  "slug": "hourly-sync",
  "cron": "0 * * * *",
  "url": "https://api.example.com/jobs/sync"
}'

# 2. Inside that endpoint: take the lock, or return early.
#    3. And ping Pulse at the end, so silence is still an alert
#       even if Relay itself stops calling.
curl -X POST "$PULSE/v1/ping/hourly-sync?interval=3600&grace=600" "\${auth[@]}"`;

const lockAcquire = `RESPONSE=$(curl -sf -X POST "$LOCK/v1/locks/nightly-import?ttl=60&owner=$HOSTNAME" \\
  "\${auth[@]}") || { echo "someone else has it"; exit 0; }

TOKEN=$(jq -r .token <<< "$RESPONSE")
trap 'curl -sf -X DELETE "$LOCK/v1/locks/nightly-import?token=$TOKEN" "\${auth[@]}" > /dev/null' EXIT

./import.sh`;

const lockRenew = `curl -X POST "$LOCK/v1/locks/nightly-import/renew?token=$TOKEN&ttl=60" "\${auth[@]}"`;

const lockStatus = `curl -s "$LOCK/v1/locks" "\${auth[@]}" | jq
# [{ "name": "nightly-import", "held": true, "owner": "pod-a", "fence": 8, "expires_at": "..." }]`;

const keys = `curl -s "$PULSE/v1/keys" "\${auth[@]}" | jq            # prefixes, last used, active
curl -X POST "$PULSE/v1/keys" "\${auth[@]}" -d '{"name":"CI"}'  # new key, shown once
curl -X DELETE "$PULSE/v1/keys/key_abc123" \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $NEW_KEY"                          # revoke the old one`;

const usage = `curl -s "$PULSE/v1/whoami" "\${auth[@]}" | jq .usage
# { "monitors": 3, "schedules": 2, "used": 5, "limit": 5, "remaining": 0 }`;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead
			title={`How to — ${SITE_TITLE}`}
			description="Copy-paste curl walkthroughs for NanoPulse heartbeat monitoring and NanoRelay scheduled calls."
		/>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<div class="page">
				<p class="eyebrow">How to</p>
				<h1>Both services, end to end, in curl.</h1>
				<p class="lede">
					Everything below is copy-paste. There is no dashboard and no SDK — if you can run
					<code>curl</code>, you have the full product.
				</p>

				<nav class="toc">
					<a href="#setup">Setup</a>
					<a href="#pulse">Pulse: watch a job</a>
					<a href="#prove">Prove the alert works</a>
					<a href="#relay">Relay: run a job</a>
					<a href="#lock">Lock: run it once</a>
					<a href="#together">All three together</a>
					<a href="#keys">Keys and quota</a>
				</nav>

				<h2 id="setup">Setup</h2>
				<p>
					Get a key by mailing <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> or pinging
					<a href={X_URL} rel="noopener" target="_blank">{X_HANDLE}</a> on X. Then, in the shell you
					are about to work in:
				</p>
				<pre><code>{setup}</code></pre>
				<p class="muted">
					Both hostnames serve the same API, so <code>$PULSE</code> and <code>$RELAY</code> are
					interchangeable. They are separate only so the URLs read correctly in your own scripts.
				</p>

				<h2 id="pulse">Pulse: get told when a job stops running</h2>
				<p>
					The first ping creates the monitor. <code>interval</code> is how often you expect it,
					<code>grace</code> is how late it may be before we call it down — both in seconds, and
					both only read when the monitor is created.
				</p>
				<pre><code>{firstPing}</code></pre>

				<p>In real life that line lives at the end of the job:</p>
				<pre><code>{crontab}</code></pre>
				<p class="muted">
					The <code>&amp;&amp;</code> is the whole design. If the script exits non-zero the ping
					never happens, the deadline passes, and you hear about it. You never have to remember to
					report failures — failure is the default.
				</p>

				<p>A ping can carry up to 2 KB of context, which shows up in the monitor's history:</p>
				<pre><code>{pingPayload}</code></pre>

				<p>Send alerts somewhere. Any HTTPS webhook works; Slack needs no configuration:</p>
				<pre><code>{alerts}</code></pre>

				<p>Look at what happened — status, last ping, and the most recent pings and events:</p>
				<pre><code>{inspect}</code></pre>

				<p>
					If the job knows it failed, it can say so instead of waiting for the deadline. This marks
					the monitor down immediately:
				</p>
				<pre><code>{reportFailure}</code></pre>

				<p>Going on holiday with the job disabled? Pause it so it does not page you:</p>
				<pre><code>{pauseMonitor}</code></pre>

				<h2 id="prove">Prove the alert actually works</h2>
				<p>
					Worth doing once, on the day you set it up, rather than discovering the webhook was wrong
					during a real outage. <code>/v1/checks/run</code> is the same sweep the cron runs every
					minute, triggered by hand so you do not have to wait for the window to pass on its own:
				</p>
				<pre><code>{proveIt}</code></pre>
				<p class="muted">
					Then check <code>recent_events</code> on the monitor: <code>notified: true</code> means the
					receiver acknowledged the alert with a 2xx. <code>false</code> means it was not delivered.
				</p>

				<h2 id="relay">Relay: have the job run for you</h2>
				{
					!relayIsLive && (
						<p class="notice">
							<span class="tag">Planned</span>
							Relay is built but not yet serving traffic. The calls below are exactly what it
							exposes — they will work the day it goes live.
						</p>
					)
				}
				<p>
					A schedule is a cron expression, a timezone and a request. The timezone is the point:
					<code>0 3 * * *</code> in <code>Europe/Oslo</code> stays at 03:00 local across daylight
					saving, instead of drifting an hour twice a year the way a UTC-only cron does.
				</p>
				<pre><code>{createSchedule}</code></pre>

				<p>Run it immediately to check the target is reachable. This does not move the schedule:</p>
				<pre><code>{runNow}</code></pre>
				<pre><code>{runNowOutput}</code></pre>

				<p>Every run is kept — outcome, status code, duration, how many attempts it took:</p>
				<pre><code>{scheduleHistory}</code></pre>

				<p>Change it, pause it, remove it:</p>
				<pre><code>{pauseSchedule}</code></pre>

				<p class="muted">
					A failing run is retried up to <code>max_attempts</code> (max 3) with 2s and 6s backoff.
					You are alerted on the transition into failure and again on recovery — not once per failed
					run. Requests carry <code>X-NanoRelay-Run-Id</code>, and delivery is at-least-once, so make
					the endpoint idempotent.
				</p>

				<h2 id="lock">Lock: make sure it only runs once</h2>
				<p>
					Acquiring returns 201 with a token, or 409 if somebody else holds it. Because
					<code>curl -sf</code> exits non-zero on 409, the second instance just stops — and the
					<code>trap</code> releases the lock however the script ends, crash included. Capture the
					response before parsing it: if you pipe curl into <code>jq</code>, the <code>||</code>
					checks jq's exit status rather than curl's, and a refused lock silently becomes an empty
					token and a job that runs anyway.
				</p>
				<pre><code>{lockAcquire}</code></pre>

				<p>
					For a job that runs longer than its lease, renew while you work rather than asking for a
					huge TTL:
				</p>
				<pre><code>{lockRenew}</code></pre>

				<p>See who holds what, without anyone's token being revealed:</p>
				<pre><code>{lockStatus}</code></pre>
				<p class="muted">
					Only the holder can release or renew — a wrong token gets 409. The <code>fence</code>
					number increases with every acquisition and never goes backwards; hand it to whatever you
					are protecting if you need to reject a stalled holder that wakes up late.
				</p>

				<h2 id="together">All three together</h2>
				<p>
					Relay makes sure the job runs, Lock makes sure it runs once, and Pulse notices if it stops
					running at all.
				</p>
				<pre><code>{both}</code></pre>

				<h2 id="keys">Keys and quota</h2>
				<p>The API key is the whole account — there is no login, so key management is an API too:</p>
				<pre><code>{keys}</code></pre>
				<p class="muted">
					Revoking the key you are currently using is refused, so nobody can lock themselves out.
					Create the new one first, then revoke the old one with it.
				</p>

				<p>Monitors and schedules share one quota. Check where you stand:</p>
				<pre><code>{usage}</code></pre>
				<p class="muted">
					Hitting the limit refuses <em>new</em> monitors and schedules with
					<code>quota_exceeded</code>. Pings and runs for things that already exist are never
					refused for quota reasons.
				</p>
			</div>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

<style>
	.page {
		max-width: 46em;
		padding: 2em 0 0;
	}
	.lede {
		color: var(--muted);
		font-size: 1.05rem;
	}
	.toc {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		gap: 0.5em 1.2em;
		margin: 2em 0 1em;
		padding: 1em 1.2em;
		border: 1px solid var(--border);
		border-radius: var(--radius);
		background: var(--surface);
		font-size: 0.92rem;
	}
	h2 {
		margin-top: 2.4em;
		font-size: 1.5rem;
		scroll-margin-top: 4em;
	}
	.page p {
		color: var(--muted);
	}
	pre {
		margin: 1em 0 1.6em;
	}
	.notice {
		display: flex;
		align-items: baseline;
		gap: 0.8em;
		padding: 0.9em 1.1em;
		border: 1px solid var(--border);
		border-radius: var(--radius);
		background: var(--surface);
		font-size: 0.93rem;
	}
</style>
